WEBVTT

00:00:00.602 --> 00:00:29.042
<v Riley>Morning, good to have you. Sunday's the day we turn to Sky and Cosmos, and today we've got a fungus nearly five pounds heavy with no known relatives, Homo erectus in southern China about 200,000 years later than we'd thought, and the Event Horizon Telescope image of M87 re-made by a team in Australia. Plus a deep dive on a planet whose transits stopped, and a question about who writes the note when a long record goes quiet. Stay with us.

00:00:42.517 --> 00:01:03.117
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities, I'm Riley. It's Sunday, November 22nd, 2026. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:37.211
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking at what a long record can and can't tell us about the sky, the water and the land. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's mainly clear and minus 5, with a high of 3 and a low of minus 6 today, turning foggy through the day with no precipitation expected. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is minus 7 under overcast, Williams Lake is minus 5 and foggy, Quesnel is minus 6 and partly cloudy, and Riske Creek is minus 6 under overcast. Heads up if you're heading into Williams Lake this morning, because the fog is thick enough to slow the drive.

00:01:37.595 --> 00:01:41.099
<v Riley>On to the news, and it starts closer to home than the rest of it.

00:01:42.499 --> 00:02:32.777
<v Riley>Most of today's news is about things that stayed put long enough to be measured, and the first item is about keeping people warm this winter. 100 Mile Ford is taking part in the KOT Auto Group's eighth annual Coat Drive, its third year in the drive. The dealership is collecting coats, toques, mitts, gloves and scarves, and it's on track to raise over 100 jackets for community members in need. The donations go to the Cariboo Christian Life Fellowship church in the 108 Mile Ranch area at the end of November. As of November 18th, the Ford Ranger holding the donations was half full. Carole Velne, an accountant with the dealership, says they pile everything into the truck and drop it off all at once. Community members can bring donations to 100 Mile Ford during regular hours.

00:02:34.204 --> 00:03:11.826
<v Casey>Former B.C. attorney general Niki Sharma is asking the incoming Conservative government to keep backing Criminal Code changes for AI accountability. Her statement came on Thursday, November 19th, after Mother Jones published the latest part of a five-part series on mass shooters and their use of AI chatbots. That installment details the ChatGPT logs of the Tumbler Ridge shooter in the lead-up to the February 10th attack. Sharma is also asking premier-designate Lorne Doerkson to confirm his government will see the provincial lawsuit against Sam Altman and OpenAI through. She says the goal is a clear path to human and corporate accountability for what AI does.

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:54.567
<v Riley>Scientists have re-dated the Taung Child, the fossil first found in South Africa in 1924 that helped establish Africa as the origin of humanity. A Nature study led by Gabriele Macho of Vrije Universiteit Brussel dates it to between 2.96 and 3.03 million years ago. The team argues the human family tree in southern Africa was more branched than assumed, and that some fossils grouped under one species may belong to separate branches. The Taung Child remains the only fossil that can be identified with certainty as Australopithecus africanus, because no firm criteria were ever set for assigning later fossils to that species.

00:03:55.991 --> 00:04:26.333
<v Casey>Researchers in China's Yunnan Province have described Dianjunus rex, a giant fungus up to 20 inches tall and nearly 5 pounds. The name draws on the ancient Dian Kingdom and the Chinese word for fungus. It forms an entirely new biological family with no known close relatives. Ascomycetes are the largest fungal phylum, with more than 64,000 known species, and they don't form caps and stems. Instead they form fruiting bodies that release spores, and this one produces the largest mass of fruiting bodies on record.

00:04:26.783 --> 00:04:30.327
<v Riley>Nearly five pounds of fungus, and no cousins to compare it with.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:05:03.750
<v Riley>A Nature study modelled future drought alongside rising temperatures and found that most of nearly 1,000 cactus species will lose habitat as the climate changes. More than a quarter of those species could lose at least 80 percent of their habitat by 2100. Michiel Pillet, the lead author at the University of South Florida, says cacti are less drought-proof than the public assumes. Habitat loss and collection poaching were already pressuring the group before drought was added to the list.

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:42.161
<v Casey>Two Nature studies published on November 18th report that Homo erectus lived in southern China much later than the known record. Nine fossil teeth from the Naxian and Baxian caves in Guangxi date to between 324,000 and 184,000 years ago. Eight are Homo erectus, which extends the known mainland Asian record by roughly 200,000 years. Chemical evidence from the teeth and animal fossils points to a rainforest habitat. A ninth tooth matches none of the known human groups, and researchers can't yet say whether it belonged to a Denisovan or another hominin. Sci.news and Live Science both carry the story.

00:05:42.611 --> 00:05:48.963
<v Riley>Rainforest is the surprise. ScienceAlert notes the species was long linked to arid grassland and savanna.

00:05:50.431 --> 00:06:23.805
<v Casey>Paleontologists have formally named Vampyrocamelus welshi, a long-legged, giraffe-like camel that lived on the southern Great Plains about 13 million years ago. Researchers examined more than 100 catalogued bones, and the type skull came from Donley County, Texas. Many of the fossils had sat in museum collections for decades before anyone looked at them closely. The male skulls have upper canines approaching 5 centimetres and enlarged first premolars. The authors suggest the teeth may have played a part in competition for mates, though how they were used is still uncertain.

00:06:25.335 --> 00:06:45.807
<v Riley>Researchers recorded brainwaves and eye movements from dozens of humans and two macaques as they watched Despicable Me and nature documentaries. The brain stays busy during a film, and the work focuses on how unexpected scene content changes the way each glance is processed. The study is about how primate brains make sense of what they see.

00:06:47.398 --> 00:07:30.620
<v Casey>A global analysis published in Nature finds that glaciers ending in lakes lost ice faster from 2000 to 2020 than glaciers ending on land or at sea. Lake-ending glaciers lost an average of 0.66 metres of water equivalent a year, against 0.40 for land-ending and 0.36 for marine-ending. The number of lakes in direct contact with glaciers rose by 3,567, a 35 percent increase. University of Cambridge natural-hazards researcher Maximillian Van Wyk de Vries calls for a tiered system. It starts with satellite screening, moves to detailed assessments and ground monitoring, and includes lake lowering where warranted. He also calls for sustained funding and local early-warning and evacuation plans.

00:07:32.175 --> 00:07:59.767
<v Riley>A University of Bath study estimates that human metabolism produced about 2.05 billion metric tons of carbon dioxide a year by 2015, a 74 percent increase from 1985. The researchers found most of the rise came from population growth, not from each person producing more. The study also treats this breathing-based CO2 as different from fossil-fuel emissions, which matters when people compare the two.

00:08:01.212 --> 00:08:33.242
<v Casey>Sediment cores drilled from lake beds across the Amazon show the forest had virtually no wildfires through the last glacial period. Fire only became regular after Homo sapiens arrived. Satellites recorded an estimated 170,000 fires across the Amazon in the summer and fall of 1987. Popular Mechanics reports that this season's peak El Niño, arriving during the forest's dry season, could push the rainforest to a breaking point. Its plants never evolved defences against fire, and drought from climate change has made the threat worse.

00:08:33.691 --> 00:08:38.963
<v Riley>Fire is a familiar word here, but the Amazon has no fire-adapted forests to fall back on.

00:08:40.429 --> 00:09:00.573
<v Casey>Nautilus has a piece on the X Club, a group of Victorian-era British scientists who pushed universities toward independent research. Cambridge introduced sabbaticals in October 1926, letting academics take every seventh term off to do original work. The article traces universities that once trained clergy into places that also produced research.

00:09:02.042 --> 00:09:29.402
<v Riley>Eric Warrant of Lund University led a Nature study showing that Earth's magnetic field guides Australia's bogong moths on migrations of up to 1,000 kilometres. Each moth makes the trip once, to the same few caves in the Australian Alps. Last year the team showed the moths can navigate by starlight alone. This study shows the magnetic field steps in when starlight is blocked, which happens more often than a total absence of stars.

00:09:29.852 --> 00:09:33.348
<v Casey>The same field your truck compass reads. The moths got there first.

00:09:35.039 --> 00:10:06.781
<v Casey>NASA's Webb telescope observed AT 2025abao in the Andromeda galaxy 47 days after the outburst peaked. In this system a smaller star spiralled into a red giant and merged with it over about seven orbits, or seven years. The giant had about seven times the Sun's mass, and its companion about 0.7 times. The findings, published in Nature on November 18th, suggest energy transfer during the shared-envelope phase is more complicated than standard models describe. The researchers say continued observations are needed to confirm the merger.

00:10:07.231 --> 00:10:15.183
<v Riley>New Scientist reports that Jason Nordhaus at the Rochester Institute of Technology thinks nearly a third of stars may go through that phase.

00:10:16.914 --> 00:10:50.024
<v Riley>Four Australian researchers, led at CSIRO by postdoctoral fellow Samuel Lai, wrote their own imaging algorithm and ran it on the 2019 Event Horizon Telescope data for the M87 black hole. The original image combined eight telescopes in places including Chile, the United States and Antarctica. Lai's team used closed triplets, three telescopes at a time, to correct errors, instead of pairs. Their result, published in Nature, is a ring almost identical in size and shape to the 2019 image. The Register reported the story.

00:10:51.163 --> 00:11:20.281
<v Casey>A University of Sydney-led study by Hillary Davis finds that masers assigned to the Milky Way's Norma spiral arm line up with a shock front driven by the galaxy's central bar. That suggests part of the supposed arm may be a star-forming region along the bar. The team compared maser observations with large-scale simulations and estimates the bar's half-length at about 4.3 kiloparsecs. Phys.org, scimex and nature.com all carry it. Davis says dust and our position inside the galaxy make the bar hard to observe.

00:11:21.532 --> 00:11:48.340
<v Riley>An Oregon State University team published a baseline recipe in npj Microgravity for growing potatoes in simulated moon soil. Real lunar regolith is nutrient-poor and carries higher levels of toxic heavy metals. The researchers mixed it with vermicompost from red wiggler worms and other additions to make it workable. Potatoes give a lot of calories per unit of land, which is why they're on the list for future lunar colonies.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:51.878
<v Casey>Simulated soil is the start. Real regolith is the test.

00:11:52.328 --> 00:11:59.992
<v Riley>Most of what we just covered got measured, re-checked, or both, and that leads into a local group that teaches people to watch.

00:12:01.392 --> 00:12:09.168
<v Riley>Quick meta moment before we move on. This week the show's own machinery got some tweaks, and a few of them are things you'd actually hear.

00:12:09.726 --> 00:12:15.814
<v Casey>Good, because I want to hear them. First one on my list: Williams Lake now leads the local block every single day.

00:12:16.387 --> 00:12:26.091
<v Riley>Right, #569. Before, the local stories floated wherever they scored. Now Williams Lake gets the top slot in that block every day, so you'll hear it first.

00:12:26.633 --> 00:12:33.465
<v Casey>And that's a fair thing to want. But who decides it doesn't get stale by year five? A fixed lead slot can turn into wallpaper.

00:12:34.137 --> 00:12:41.857
<v Riley>That's a fair worry, and it's a guardrail rather than a guarantee. It sets the opening, but the stories underneath still get picked on merit.

00:12:42.543 --> 00:12:47.647
<v Casey>Okay, I'll allow it. Next: the weather. Environment Canada now stands in when Open-Meteo fails.

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:58.676
<v Riley>Yes, #580. Open-Meteo is the primary source, and when it's unreachable the show falls back to Environment Canada's numbers instead of going silent on the forecast.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:09.820
<v Casey>Now that one I like, but I want the caveat said plainly. It's a fallback. It isn't guaranteed to hold every episode, and I'd rather listeners know that than assume the forecast is always covered.

00:13:10.113 --> 00:13:13.753
<v Riley>Agreed, and that's fair. It covers the failure case, nothing more.

00:13:14.263 --> 00:13:19.935
<v Casey>Then the correction change. Every point of a production correction now airs, rather than being trimmed.

00:13:20.461 --> 00:13:27.989
<v Riley>#574. Which I think is the right call. A correction that quietly drops half its points isn't really a correction.

00:13:28.561 --> 00:13:36.849
<v Casey>Hard disagree on the phrasing, though. Airing every point can make a small fix sound like a bigger scandal than it was. Thoroughness isn't always clarity.

00:13:37.498 --> 00:13:43.106
<v Riley>That's a real tension, and I'll grant it. But I'd still rather over-explain a mistake than hide part of it.

00:13:43.421 --> 00:13:47.573
<v Casey>Fine. Last one: the Council of Canadians is now a news source feeding the show.

00:13:48.083 --> 00:13:55.835
<v Riley>That's a new source upstream, so it changes which stories reach us before we ever pick from them. You'll see more of their coverage in the mix.

00:13:56.499 --> 00:14:01.075
<v Casey>Good. I'll want to see who they're actually covering, not just that they're in the feed.

00:14:01.706 --> 00:14:07.890
<v Riley>Fair, and that's a test we should keep applying as the list grows. Let's move on to the rest of the show.

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:35.402
<v Riley>The Cariboo Chilcotin Conservation Society is an environmental education nonprofit that has been at it since 1996. Its programs include Water Wise, Waste Wise, Skates for All, and school field programs that take students outside to see the land they live on. If your school or group wants to know what's on offer, find them at conservationsociety.ca. It's the kind of local work that builds the habit of paying attention.

00:14:35.758 --> 00:14:40.470
<v Casey>Programs that have run since 1996 are the ones I trust more than any launch.

00:14:40.773 --> 00:14:43.877
<v Riley>Which brings us to the deep dive, and a question about silence.

00:14:45.277 --> 00:15:15.669
<v Riley>When a long-running watcher goes quiet, is the silence the finding, or just a hole the record has to own up to? And when the watcher is a camera on a creek that a neighbour runs, what does the silence say about the neighbours? Start with TOI-2535 b. NASA's TESS satellite watched this hot Jupiter from April 2019 to March 2026. The planet is about 1,000 light-years away and circles its star every 8.25 days. Then the transits stopped.

00:15:15.849 --> 00:15:16.465
<v Casey>Stopped how?

00:15:16.774 --> 00:15:29.150
<v Riley>They had been arriving up to six hours early or late, and they were getting shorter and shallower. The study attributes that to the orbit tilting out of our line of sight, pulled by a nearby planet nobody has seen yet.

00:15:29.501 --> 00:15:33.101
<v Casey>So the unseen planet is doing the explaining. That's the weak spot.

00:15:33.731 --> 00:15:51.755
<v Riley>It's inferred from timing, and an analysis using the NEID spectrograph at Arizona's WIYN telescope points to a companion. That's a prediction, and predictions can fail. The companion could transit sooner, even though the hot Jupiter itself isn't expected to transit again for at least 30 years.

00:15:52.388 --> 00:15:58.604
<v Casey>Points to, though. Nobody has found it. And the study says how the planet got that close to its star is still uncertain.

00:15:59.284 --> 00:16:20.796
<v Riley>That's an open part of the record, not a hole in it. There's precedent for the mechanism too. Kepler-413b stopped transiting for 800 days under the influence of its two stars. TOI-1518b's orbit is expected to shift because of its star's shape. So disappearing transits have happened before, and astronomers have explanations for them.

00:16:21.378 --> 00:16:31.634
<v Casey>Kepler-413b is a mechanism we've watched work for 800 days. TOI-2535 b is a mechanism we've inferred from its shadow. Those aren't the same level of proof.

00:16:32.166 --> 00:16:49.574
<v Riley>They're the same kind of evidence, though. Both depend on a long baseline. Samuel Yee, the study's corresponding author, says we think of the universe as clockwork, where everything is regular, and that's not true. You only notice the regularity breaking if you've recorded the regularity for years.

00:16:50.242 --> 00:16:57.954
<v Casey>Seven years of dips is the record. The explanation is a story laid on top of it, and the story can be wrong while the record stays right.

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:05.182
<v Riley>Agreed, and that's the useful split. The record is the thing we can check. The companion is the thing we can test.

00:17:05.868 --> 00:17:13.812
<v Casey>Then test it before you call it a discovery. The press coverage went straight to "invisible planet" and skipped the part where the planet is a guess.

00:17:14.104 --> 00:17:16.552
<v Riley>The headlines did skip it. The paper didn't.

00:17:17.147 --> 00:17:23.275
<v Casey>A paper saying "inferred" and a headline saying "found" reach different readers, and only one of them checks the sky.

00:17:23.896 --> 00:17:53.800
<v Riley>Now the other kind of silence. Voyager 1 has sent no science or engineering data since November 14th. Its carrier signal is still detectable, and on November 18th the team used that carrier to confirm the probe had reached one light-day from Earth, roughly 16 billion miles out. The carrier is the plain tone. The data rides on it, and frame sync is the marker that tells the receiver where each block starts. The spacecraft still has the tone and has lost the marker.

00:17:54.407 --> 00:18:02.959
<v Casey>Still transmitting is not the same as doing science. Every command takes a day to get out there and a day to come back. Nobody is sending a repair crew.

00:18:03.547 --> 00:18:25.731
<v Riley>No crew, but a remote team that has fixed this kind of thing before. A similar interruption was traced to an onboard computer, and the science data came back in June 2024. Project manager Suzanne Dodd called this an anomaly that is not something unseen before. She also said she is confident it won't be too long before the probe sends data again.

00:18:26.407 --> 00:18:34.911
<v Casey>Confident is a word people use before the second fault. Dodd also expects more anomalies as the probes age. Those two statements sit together uneasily.

00:18:35.536 --> 00:18:53.264
<v Riley>They sit together because the team is working the problem and expects the machine to keep giving them problems. That's a maintenance schedule, not a crisis. Silence comes in degrees here. The carrier confirms the craft is transmitting and where it is. The missing piece is the readings.

00:18:53.928 --> 00:19:13.000
<v Casey>The carrier confirms distance. It doesn't give us the readings, and the readings are the reason the mission exists. Linda Spilker, the project scientist at JPL, said scientists haven't yet determined where the Sun's influence ends and undisturbed interstellar space begins. That's the thing we're waiting on, and we can't get it while the data is down.

00:19:13.603 --> 00:19:29.387
<v Riley>Nicky Fox at NASA put it more plainly. The probes are in a region no spacecraft has explored directly, so every measurement they return is valuable. That makes this silence costly, and it's still a fault under investigation. Label the gap by what is still known.

00:19:29.908 --> 00:19:40.708
<v Casey>That changes how I'd write it. "Fault under investigation, carrier confirmed, science unknown" is an honest entry. "No data" is a blank, and a blank invites people to fill it with whatever they fear.

00:19:41.396 --> 00:20:11.084
<v Riley>Good, we agree on the entry. The lakes are the third kind of silence, and they're not silence at all. A PNAS study led by Ke Zhang and colleagues looked at long-term records from about 2,000 lakes. It finds that temperature, algal blooms and oxygen levels are increasingly moving together across continents. The authors call this an emergent property of the Anthropocene. Lakes normally buffer each other's shocks, because each one responds differently to the same event.

00:20:11.653 --> 00:20:22.093
<v Casey>And the coverage calls it a warning, while the authors call it a trend with a possible interpretation. A satellite can see a lake's surface. It can't see what's happening under the ice in February.

00:20:22.761 --> 00:20:35.169
<v Riley>Satellites can see the surface across 2,000 lakes at once, and no field crew can do that. Synchrony is the thing a single lake can't show you. You need the whole set to see the pattern at all.

00:20:35.741 --> 00:20:48.397
<v Casey>Synchrony can also come from shared drivers. Regional weather and warming air would push lakes in the same direction without any new fragility at all. Then this is a sign of climate forcing, not proof of a new system-level risk.

00:20:49.008 --> 00:21:03.440
<v Riley>Shared forcing or not, the result is the same for management. The coverage notes that lakes are mainly managed locally or regionally. If they move together, a local manager who only watches one lake will miss the signal that the whole set is shifting.

00:21:04.065 --> 00:21:09.865
<v Casey>So the global signal needs local readers. Who reads the local record for Horsefly Lake when the satellite passes over?

00:21:10.208 --> 00:21:18.880
<v Riley>Right now, nobody is set up to reconcile the two. That's the gap the study points at, and it's a gap in who's reading, not in what's being measured.

00:21:19.561 --> 00:21:25.673
<v Casey>I'd like the paper to say that more clearly. The reading layer is where the cost lives, and nobody budgets for it.

00:21:26.293 --> 00:21:38.213
<v Riley>Take a dark-sky count. It has three possible entries: a clear night with no aurora, a cloudy night, and a night nobody went out. A row of zeros can't tell those apart unless someone marks which one it was.

00:21:38.885 --> 00:21:51.941
<v Casey>And it's the same question as the creek camera. Mark the gap or the gap writes itself. A wildlife camera on a creek records the animals, and it also records the people who walk past, the trucks that come in, and the hours they keep.

00:21:52.530 --> 00:22:08.162
<v Riley>It records the animals too, and that's the part nobody argues about. A camera on a creek can tell you when the salmon arrive, when the bears come down to drink, whether the water is cooling in a dry August. That's ecological truth that a single visit can't give you.

00:22:08.752 --> 00:22:21.008
<v Casey>Valuable to whom? A neighbour's camera pointed at a creek crossing knows whose truck goes past at six in the morning. That's a public record of who's around, and nobody consented to it except the person who mounted the camera.

00:22:21.616 --> 00:22:36.312
<v Riley>Then set the rule before the camera goes up. Decide what the camera is for. Keep the animal frames, limit the human frames, set how long anything is kept, and publish the rule with the data. That turns a surveillance device into a monitoring station.

00:22:36.866 --> 00:22:42.586
<v Casey>Who checks that the rule is followed? A neighbour can promise to delete the frames. Nobody can audit that from the road.

00:22:43.102 --> 00:22:52.686
<v Riley>A body that holds the log, with the people living along the creek able to see it. I don't have the name of that body for the Cariboo, and I'm not going to invent one.

00:22:53.309 --> 00:22:58.429
<v Casey>Good. Don't invent one. The marks also cost time, and nobody puts that in a grant announcement.

00:22:58.989 --> 00:23:21.549
<v Riley>The cost is real, and it's the same whether the log is a camera or a sky count. Three things belong beside any long record: the expected pattern, the known gaps, and the reason for each gap. TESS's anomaly was readable only because the expected transit times were known. Voyager's team separated the carrier from the missing science, and that let them report the outage honestly.

00:23:22.125 --> 00:23:30.181
<v Casey>And who writes the reason? A gap note written by the same people who lost the data is a self-report. You're asking the team to grade its own outage.

00:23:30.707 --> 00:23:38.771
<v Riley>An outside check helps. The companion could also show up sooner than the 30-year wait for the planet, so the test doesn't have to wait that long.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:47.772
<v Casey>Could. I accept that a sooner test exists. A companion that transits in five years still leaves the question of who wrote down the gap in the meantime.

00:23:48.312 --> 00:23:53.816
<v Riley>Then we agree the record has to say what it couldn't see. We disagree about who should write it.

00:23:54.329 --> 00:24:00.425
<v Casey>The person who kept the log can't be the only one writing the note. That part is settled for me, and the rest isn't.

00:24:01.093 --> 00:24:15.333
<v Riley>A body that holds the log is one fix. Another is a reader who gets to check the note against the raw data. Either could work, though I can't say which fits a town of a few thousand people, where the person with the camera is also your neighbour.

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:22.196
<v Casey>Nor can I, and that's unproven. It's why I'd want the rule written down before anyone has a reason to argue about it.

00:24:22.756 --> 00:24:45.956
<v Riley>So the question stays open. When a long record of the sky, the land or the water goes quiet, who writes the note that says why? The person who kept it, a body that holds it, or a later reader who checks it? And how do you tell a quiet sky from a night nobody was watching? If you have a story tip or a sky-count log you'd like to share, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:24:46.307 --> 00:24:49.219
<v Casey>Mind the fog on the roads, and enjoy the rest of your Sunday.

00:24:49.819 --> 00:25:08.971
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.